# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/

# Intermediate per-composite cache: 78 .npz, ~14 MB.
# Purely a build accelerator -- fully regenerable from the pipeline, and a
# fresh clone skips it by re-downloading from Planetary Computer instead.
data/cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build caches are NOT tracked. They are regenerable and enormous, and they
# are not what the viewer reads:
#
# data/mosaic_cache/   the per-bin 30 m mosaic cache: 15.3 GB for 2017 alone
#                       (664 MB a bin), and ~406 GB for the full record.
# data/cache_regional/ the per-cell composite and change caches: 70 MB.
#
# data/regional/ IS tracked, deliberately -- it is the published archive the
# viewers read, 61.6 MiB for 2017, and it is what GitHub Pages serves.
# Rebuild everything with:
#
#   PYTHONPATH=src python -m h3lab.run --regional --year 2017 --resolutions 7,8
#
# The stages are cached independently, so the network-bound part (mosaic) is
# paid once and everything after it is arithmetic over files on disk. Drop the
# mosaic cache on any machine that does not intend to rebuild: it is 15 GB for
# one year and the archive is 62 MiB.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
data/mosaic_cache/
data/cache_regional/
data/regional_test/

# Scratch: calibration prototypes and one-off measurements. The measurement
# scripts that back the numbers in docs/ live in tools/measure/ and are
# tracked.
data/_t/

# Build logs
logs/

# OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# data/rookery_bay_h3_meta.json and data/*.bin.gz ARE tracked, deliberately.
#
# They are derived, but at 14 MB total they are 0.03% of GitHub's 5 GB repo
# ceiling and 11% of the 100 MB per-file limit. Tracking them means a fresh
# clone can open the viewer immediately instead of running a one-hour,
# network-dependent pipeline first.
#
# The exporter sets gzip mtime=0, so re-exporting unchanged data yields
# byte-identical files and git reports no diff. If you ever see these change in
# `git status`, the underlying data genuinely changed -- check it before
# committing rather than reaching for `git add -A`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
